rules:
1 The Life Code — issued by the Council of Australian Life Insurers (CALI); the current edition took effect on 1 Mar 2025 (cl 1.4). It covers life cover with terminal illness, TPD, trauma, income protection, business expenses, funeral and consumer credit insurance from life insurers (cl 1.7), and doesn't bind advice businesses, advisers or super trustees unless they adopt it (cl 1.10). An independent review reported on 30 Jun 2026 with 85 recommendations and CALI published an action plan on 30 Sep 2026, but no new Code text has been issued, so proposals such as new claim-reopening timeframes aren't in force.
2 Claim decisions (cl 5.47–5.60) — income-related claims (income protection, business expenses): decide within 2 months of the Claim Received Date, or of the end of the waiting period if that is later (cl 5.48). Lump-sum claims (death, terminal illness, trauma, TPD): within 6 months (cl 5.49). Written decision within 15 business days of receiving all the information needed (cl 5.50). If a delay is beyond the insurer's control, written notice before the 2- or 6-month mark and updates every 20 business days (cl 5.59), with escalation to a senior officer or committee after 12 months (cl 5.60). A reopened claim gets a new Claim Received Date (cl 5.57).
3 Keeping the claimant informed — within 10 business days of receiving a claim, explain the Code, the cover, any waiting period, the benefits and the claims process (cl 5.5); progress updates at least every 20 business days (cl 5.6); reply to requests for information within 10 business days (cl 5.7); ask for medical and financial reports within 20 business days (cl 5.55); pay income protection benefits by the due date or within 5 business days of receiving all the information (cl 5.63); limit surveillance to 4 months (cl 5.42(h)); give 3 months' notice before a benefit period ends, a definition changes or benefits reduce (cl 5.8–5.10). For accepted claims of $25,000 or more that aren't income-related, suggest independent financial advice, unless the benefit is paid to a super trustee (cl 5.61).
4 Vulnerability — insurers must respond to vulnerability, including age, disability, mental health, language, remote location, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander status, family violence and financial distress (cl 6.1); recognise a support person — a lawyer, consumer representative, interpreter, family member, carer or friend — in all reasonable ways, with interpreters arranged and paid for by the insurer (cl 6.13); offer financial hardship options (cl 6.18); and respond to a request for urgent access to benefits within 5 business days of receiving the evidence (cl 6.25–6.27).
5 Complaints — the insurer's final written response is due within 30 calendar days (cl 7.13), in line with ASIC RG 271 for standard complaints. A complaint about how a claim is handled is a standard complaint (RG 271.32(d)), and a complaint about a declined claim or the value of a claim needs a written response even if it is resolved within 5 business days (RG 271.75).
6 APRA's income protection measures (letter of 30 Sep 2020; APRA expectations enforced through capital requirements, not legislation):
• no new agreed-value income protection from 31 Mar 2020 — benefits must be based on income at the time of the claim;
• from 1 Oct 2021, stable incomes are covered for annual earnings at the time of the claim event, no older than 12 months, and variable incomes on average earnings over a period suited to the occupation;
• for new policies from 1 Oct 2021, all benefits combined can't exceed 90% of earnings for the first 6 months of a claim and 70% after that; claim payments can be indexed only by a suitable inflation index; rehabilitation or retraining payments and super contributions can be paid on top;
• the 5-year contract-term measure was suspended on 24 Mar 2022;
• there is no prescribed switch from an own-occupation to an any-occupation definition — insurers set their own controls for long benefit periods, and the Code requires 3 months' notice and an offer to reassess before a definition changes (cl 5.9).
7 Worked example (income protection, policy issued after 1 Oct 2021): insured monthly benefit $7,000; average earnings in the 12 months before the disability $8,000 a month; the policy pays the lesser of the insured benefit and 70% of those earnings = $5,600 a month. The benefit is taxable income.
8 How Life Advice Guru supports a claim — Ben's agent checks the policy, the definition, the waiting period, exclusions and the evidence, builds the claim pack and calculates the expected benefit with the working shown; Theo approves lodging every claim through the Teams approval card, and every death or terminal illness claim; Ben lodges the claim and tracks the insurer against the Code; Cara keeps the client or family informed in plain English; vulnerable clients have one named contact.
